mplies deeper first. For equal depth, we need to decide a tie-break. The instruction says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" It doesn't specify ascending or descending. But \"stable tie break\" suggests deterministic ordering. Usually alphabetical order works. We'll assume ascending order (since smaller ID? But IDs are like \"n1\",\"n2\", etc. Could use string compare).\n\nNow, for a node to be removed, the following conditions must hold:\n- Node is removable (true)\n- Node is not root (parent is not None)\n- Node's symbol is not in required_symbols\n- Node has no children that are retained (i.e., none of its children are in the set of retained nodes after processing)\n- Also, after removing node, all required symbols must still be represented in the tree (meaning that at least one node with each required symbol remains in the retained set). However, we only remove a node if it is not itself required. But removing it might remove the only occurrence of a required symbol if that symbol appears only on that node? But we already prevented removal of required symbol nodes. But a required symbol could also appear on a non-required node? Actually required_symbols are symbols that must be present in the tree at least once. If a required symbol appears only on a non-required node that is removable, removing it would lose that required symbol. The condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" means we must check after removal that there is at least one retained node with each required symbol. So we need to keep track of counts of required symbols among retained nodes. Initially, all nodes are considered retained. When we remove a node, we decrement counts for its symbol. If any required symbol count would become zero, we cannot remove that node.\n\nAlso, we must consider that removing a node might cause its parent to lose a child. But the rule \"Never remove a node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node that still has children that are retained. But if we remove a child, the parent might later become removable if it has no remaining children. So bottom-up processing works: we first consider deepest nodes. When we decide to remove a node, we should also consider that its own parent might later be evaluated. But we must not remove a node that has retained children. Since we process bottom-up, by the time we evaluate a node, its children have already been considered. If a child is removed, it's not retained. So we only check if any child remains retained (i.e., not removed). So the condition is: for each node, if any of its children are in retained set (i.e., not removed yet), then we cannot remove the node.\n\nWhen we remove a node, we should add it to removed list in the order we remove it. The removed_node_ids should be in the order of removal (the actual order we decide). Retained_node_ids should be sorted (maybe alphabetically by ID). Also we need to return both lists.\n\nEdge cases: What about nodes that are not removable (removable=false) cannot be removed even if conditions satisfied. Root is not removable anyway.\n\nPotential nuance: \"Never remove the root\" - we treat root as node with parent null. But root's removable is false.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Parse case: nodes list, required_symbols set.\n2. Build dictionary: id -> node dict. Also children list for each node (initialize empty list for each id, then for each node, if parent not None, append child id to parent's children list).\n3. Determine list of all node ids.\n4. Initialize retained set = set of all node ids. removed list = []. (We'll build removal order as we go)\n5. Maintain a count map: symbol -> number of retained nodes with that symbol. Initially compute counts for all nodes (including removable and non-removable). Required_symbols are those that must have count >0 at all times.\n6. Sort nodes to process: sort by depth descending, then by node ID (string) as tie-breaker. We'll iterate this order.\n7. For each node in that order:\n   a. Check if node is removable (node['removable'] is True). If not, skip.\n   b. Check if node's parent is not None (i.e., not root).\n   c. Check if node's symbol is not in required_symbols set. (Because if it is required, we cannot remove it)\n   d. Check if any child of this node is still in retained set. If yes, skip because node has retained children.\n   e. Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol count to drop to zero: i.e., if node's symbol is required? Already checked that symbol not required, so removing it won't affect required symbol counts. Wait: The required symbol could be present on other nodes as well. But if we remove a node with a non-required symbol, it doesn't affect required symbols count. However, what if the node's symbol is not required, but removing it might remove the only node that has a required symbol? No, required symbol is a specific symbol string. If the node's symbol is not in required_symbols, then its removal does not affect counts of required symbols. So the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" is automatically true if the node's symbol is not required. But the phrase \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" could be interpreted as: before removing, ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present. That includes the case where the node itself might be required? But we already exclude required nodes. For non-required nodes, removal does not affect required symbol counts unless that node is the only occurrence of a required symbol? But if its symbol is not required, then it's irrelevant. So the condition is automatically satisfied for non-required symbols. However, there is a subtlety: what if a required symbol appears on a node that is not itself required? No, \"required_symbols\" is a list of symbol strings that must be present. The node's symbol is either required or not. If a node's symbol is required, we won't remove