retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n\nNow escape:\n\nReplace each newline with \\n and add \\ at end of lines? In JSON string, newline is \\n. Also replace backslashes: there are none except in \"lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id'])\" which has backslashes? No. Actually there is no backslash. But there are single quotes, which don't need escaping in JSON string as long as the string is delimited by double quotes. We need to escape double quotes within the string: In the return dict, we have double quotes around \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". Those need to become \\\"removed_node_ids\\\" etc. Also in the raise ValueError line, we have double quotes around \"Required symbol not present\". So we need to escape those double quotes.\n\nWe'll manually produce the escaped string.\n\nLet's build line by line:\n\nLine 1: def solve(case):\nLine 2:     nodes = case['nodes']\nLine 3:     required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n... etc.\n\nWe'll compile into a single string with \\n escapes.\n\nBetter to write it as a raw string in Python then encode with json.dumps. But since I am generating text, I'll simulate.\n\nI'll produce the final JSON response.\n\nI'll also ensure there are no extra spaces.\n\nLet's produce:\n\n{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_count = {}\\n    for node in node_map.values():\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if symbol_count.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Required symbol not present\\\")\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        keep_child = False\\n        for child_id in children[nid]:\\n            if child_id in retained:\\n                keep_child = True\\n                break\\n        if keep_child:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        symbol_count[node['symbol']] -= 1\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\"}\n\nWe need to check that the double quotes in the return dict are escaped: we used \\\"removed_node_ids\\\" and \\\"retained_node_ids\\\". Also the double quotes in raise ValueError are escaped: \\\"Required symbol not present\\\". Good.\n\nAlso need to escape any backslashes in the code? There is a backslash inside lambda? No, there is a minus sign. No backslash.\n\nCheck for any single quotes: they are fine.\n\nNow, ensure that the JSON is valid: The string must not contain unescaped control characters. Newlines are escaped as \\n. The entire string is inside double quotes.\n\nWe'll output this directly.\n\nMake sure the response does not include any extra text.\n\nWe'll output exactly the JSON object.\n\nI'll now produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":6104,"total_tokens":8276,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5693},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tok